<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Satnampanth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1657">1657</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Satnami Samaj, Satnami movement, Sadhanpanth</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Satnampanth, or the Satnami movement, is a name for several Indian religious movements influenced by Kabirpanth and connected with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalit">Dalits</a>, named after Satnam, the 'true name'. The first was founded by Bir Bhan at Narnaul in 1657; its followers, mostly farmers and traders from the low-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> leatherworkers, rejected ritual and caste distinctions. In 1672 the Satnamis rose against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> over taxation, took Narnaul and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bairat">Bairat</a> and defeated Mughal forces until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> sent 10,000 troops with artillery; 2,000 Satnamis were killed in battle and many more in pursuit. The movement revived under Jagjivan Das in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> and under Ghasidas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a> in the 1820s.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1672 the <b>Satnamis</b> of Narnaul, a sect that rejected caste and drew many followers from low-caste leatherworkers, rose against Mughal officials; the court chronicler sneered at them as goldsmiths, carpenters, sweepers and tanners.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li></ul></section>
